 To force a textfield in Adobe to display columns, you can use the TextFrame's `textLayout` property and the `TextLineMetrics` object. Here's a simplified example:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a TextField object named 'myTextField'
var textField = myTextField;

// Define your text with columns
var text = "Column 1 Data\tColumn 2 Data\tColumn 3 Data";

// Create a TextFormat object for formatting
var textFormat = new TextFormat("Arial", 12);

// Create a TextFlow and add the text with the defined format
var textFlow = new TextFlow(text.split("\t"), textFormat);

// Calculate the number of columns and the width for each column
var numColumns = 3;
var columnWidth = textField.width / numColumns;

// Create an array to store the lines
var lines = [];

// Iterate through the TextFlow and create lines
for (var i = 0; i < textFlow.numLines; i++) {
    var line = new TextLine(textFlow.getLineAt(i), textFormat);
    line.x = 0;
    line.y = i * columnWidth;
    line.height = line.ascent + line.descent + line.leading;
    line.width = columnWidth;
    lines.push(line);
}

// Set the TextFrame's textLayout with the calculated lines
textField.textLayout = new TextLineLayout(lines);
```

This script will format your text into columns in the specified textfield. Adjust the number of columns, text, and formatting as needed for your specific use case.

