 In Laravel, you can create a controller function to find a user by ID and return the result as JSON. Here's a basic example of how you might do this:

```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\User; // Import the User model
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Routing\Controller;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Validator;

class UserController extends Controller
{
    public function show($id)
    {
        // Validate the request
        $validator = Validator::make(['id' => $id], [
            'id' => 'required|exists:users,id'
        ]);

        if ($validator->fails()) {
            return response()->json($validator->errors(), 400);
        }

        // Find the user by ID and return as JSON
        $user = User::find($id);
        return response()->json($user);
    }
}
```

This function, `show`, accepts a user ID as a parameter. It first validates the provided ID to ensure it exists in the database. If the validation fails, it returns an error response with a 400 status code. If the validation passes, it finds the user with the provided ID and returns the user object as a JSON response.

This example assumes that you have a User model set up and that you have defined a route in your `routes/web.php` file to call this function, such as:

```php
Route::get('/users/{id}', 'UserController@show');
```

This route will map the URL `/users/{id}` to the `show` function in the `UserController`.

